      "synth": {
        "answer": "### Van Inwagen’s Consequence Argument\n\nPeter van Inwagen presents the **Consequence Argument** as \"the best argument\" for incompatibilism—the view that free will and moral responsibility are incompatible with causal determinism [Source: incompatibilism-arguments]. The argument is designed to show that if determinism is true, then no one has any power over the past or the laws of nature; consequently, since our actions are inevitable consequences of those factors, we have no power over them either.\n\nVan Inwagen offers an informal statement of this argument in his book *An Essay on Free Will* (1983). While the exact formal logic varies across presentations by Ginet (1966), Wiggins (1973), and van Inwagen himself, the core structure relies on a principle often labeled \"Beta\" or similar transfer principles:\n1.  No one has power over the facts of the past [Source: freewill].\n2.  No one has power over the laws of nature [Source: incompatibilism-arguments/freewill context].\n3.  If determinism is true, then our present actions are necessary consequences of the past and the laws [Source: compatibilism].\n4.  Therefore, no one has power over their present actions; we cannot do otherwise than what we actually do given the past and the laws [Source: compatibilism/incompatibilism-arguments].\n\nThe argument \"shook compatibilists\" because it provided powerful support for the claim that if determinism implies a single inevitable future, agents lack control over how that future unfolds [Source: compatibilism]. It earned incompatibilists an important dialectical advantage by challenging compatibilists to explain exactly where the reasoning fails or why our ordinary notion of ability survives this logic [Source: compatibilism].\n\n### The Strongest Compatibilist Response: David Lewis’s Critique\n\nWhile there have been numerous replies (e.g., Lehrer 1980; Slote 1982), the most influential objection is due to **David Lewis** in his 1981 paper \"The Causal Explanation of Action.\" Lewis argues that van Inwagen’s argument equivocates on the meaning of **\"is able to break a law of nature\"** [Source: freewill].\n\nLewis distinguishes between two senses of breaking a law:\n1.  **Sense A:** Acting in violation of a universal, exceptionless law (i.e., doing something that *would* be impossible if the laws were strictly followed).\n2.  **Sense B:** Performing an action such that, had you done it, the past or the laws would have been different than they actually are.\n\nVan Inwagen assumes Sense B when he claims we cannot change the past or laws. However, Lewis argues that compatibilists can accept that we lack \"regulative control\" over the past and laws (in the sense that we cannot make them false) while still maintaining that we have abilities defined by counterfactuals about our own actions [Source: freewill]. Specifically, Lewis suggests that in a deterministic world, I am able to do X if *if* I were to choose/decide to do X, then I would do X—even though determinism ensures I wouldn't decide otherwise given my actual prior state. The equivocation lies in treating \"ability\" as requiring the power to alter the antecedent conditions (past/laws), which van Inwagen denies is possible, whereas compatibilists define ability relative to the agent's internal states and choices alone.\n\n### Alternative Compatibilist Strategy: Rejecting PAP via Frankfurt Cases\n\nA second major compatibilist response does not try to save the Consequence Argument’s premise about abilities but instead attacks its underlying assumption: **Principle of Alternate Possibilities (PAP)**. PAP states that an agent is morally responsible only if they could have done otherwise [Source: alternative-possibilities/compatibilism].\n\nHarry Frankfurt developed arguments (Frankfurt-style cases) showing that moral responsibility can exist even when alternate possibilities are removed or blocked by external factors (e.g., a neuroscientist who would force Agent A to act, but doesn't need to because Agent A acts freely on their own). If PAP is false, then the incompatibilist's focus on the \"ability to do otherwise\" becomes irrelevant. Compatibilists can thus sidestep the Consequence Argument entirely by rejecting the premise that free will requires regulative control over multiple possible futures [Source: compatibilism/alternative-possibilities]. This approach grounds agency in other features of volition rather than counterfactual flexibility.\n\n### Conclusion and Remaining Tensions\n\nThe debate remains active. Van Inwagen rejects the idea that this is merely a verbal disagreement about 'can', insisting he and Lewis mean exactly what ordinary speakers mean in contexts of deliberation [Source: incompatibilism-arguments]. He argues that validity of Beta (the transfer principle) is more plausible than any specific compatibilist analysis of ability, especially since simple conditional analyses had already been rejected by many philosophers [Source: incompatibilism-arguments]. Consequently, while Lewis’s equivocation objection is widely cited as the strongest direct rebuttal to the Consequence Argument's logic, it has not fully resolved the tension for all parties; many compatibilists now prefer Frankfurt-style routes or \"sourcehood\" accounts over trying to defend regulative control directly against van Inwagen’s premises [Source: freewill/compatibilism].\n\nWant me to search for recent developments in \"New Dispositionalist\" responses to the Consequence Argument?\n\n---\n*Verification note: these statements could not be confirmed against your sources — treat them as unverified:*\n- David Lewis wrote the paper \"The Causal Explanation of Action\" in 1981.\n- The argument is designed to show that if determinism is true, then no one has any power over the past or the laws of nature; consequently, since our actions are…\n- While the exact formal logic varies across presentations by Ginet (1966), Wiggins (1973), and van Inwagen himself\n- or similar transfer principles:\n- While there have been numerous replies (e.g., Lehrer 1980; Slote 1982), the most influential objection is due to **David Lewis** in his 1981 paper",
        "reasoning_chars": 0,
