        "answer": "Chalmers’s hard problem of consciousness is the question of why and how physical processing in the brain gives rise to a rich inner life [Source: consciousness-neuroscience]. It asks \"why there is something it is like to entertain a mental image, or to experience an emotion\" when cognitive systems engage in information-processing [Source: consciousness-neuroscience]. Chalmers argues that while we can explain the structural and functional aspects of these processes (the \"easy problems\"), empirical explanations will always leave out the essential ingredient—the subjective quality itself—because phenomenal consciousness cannot be reduced to purely structural or functional descriptions [Source: consciousness-neuroscience]. This creates what he calls an \"explanatory gap,\" where one can imagine creatures with identical brain processes but completely lacking conscious experience (zombies) [Source: neuroscience; consciousness-representational].\n\nIllusionism proposes to dissolve this problem rather than solve it by arguing that the very phenomenon Chalmers is asking about does not exist. Proponents like Keith Frankish argue that introspection yields representations that substantially misrepresent our inner experience, much like sensory illusions misrepresent the outer world [Source: materialism-eliminative]. Introspection tricks us into believing we have phenomenal properties—the intrinsic \"what-it-is-likeness\" of qualitative states—but these properties are actually illusory [Source: materialism-eliminative]. \n\nInstead of solving how physical processes create non-physical qualia (which would require bridging an impossible gap), illusionists claim there are only \"quasi-phenomenal properties\"—non-phenomenal properties of inner states detected by introspection but misrepresented as having a magical subjective quality [Source: materialism-eliminative]. By eliminating the existence of true phenomenal consciousness, illusionism removes the need to explain its origin. The hard problem is dissolved because it is based on a conceptual confusion or misapprehension; we do not need to explain why brain processes produce what-it-is-likeness, because that property does not exist in reality [Source: materialism-eliminative].\n\n---\n*Verification note: these statements could not be confirmed against your sources — treat them as unverified:*\n- Chalmers argues that empirical explanations leave out the essential ingredient of subjective quality because phenomenal consciousness cannot be reduced to struc…",
        "reasoning_chars": 0,
