      "synth": {
        "answer": "Hilary Putnam’s Twin Earth thought experiment is designed to demonstrate that the meaning of a term—and by extension, the content of mental states—is not determined solely by what goes on inside an individual's mind. The scenario posits two identical planets: Earth and \"Twin Earth.\" On Earth, the clear, tasteless liquid in oceans and rivers has the chemical composition H₂O [Source: content-externalism]. On Twin Earth, which is molecule-for-molecule identical to Earth in every other respect, this same substance (which looks and tastes exactly like water) actually consists of a different chemical compound, referred to as XYZ [Source: naturalism-moral][Source: content-narrow].\n\nThe experiment focuses on an inhabitant of Earth named Oscar around 1750—a time before the discovery that water is H₂O—and his exact duplicate on Twin Earth. Because they are duplicates with identical intrinsic properties, their internal physical states are qualitatively indistinguishable [Source: content-externalism]. However, when Oscar says \"water,\" he refers to H₂O; when his twin says \"water\" (or its Twin Earth equivalent), he refers to XYZ [Source: computational-mind][Source: content-externalism]. Since the reference of the word depends on the external environment rather than just the speaker's internal state or linguistic dispositions, Putnam concludes that meaning cannot be determined by intrinsic factors alone [Source: content-narrow].\n\nThe slogan **\"meanings just ain't in the head\"** commits one to **semantic and mental content externalism**. Specifically, it asserts that the individuation of mental content is not a function solely of an individual’s internal properties. Instead, what a person means—and consequently, what their beliefs are about—depends partly on facts about their physical environment (such as whether they live on Earth with H₂O or Twin Earth with XYZ) and potentially their social/linguistic community [Source: content-externalism][Source: naturalism-moral]. If two individuals have identical \"narrow\" contents (contents determined entirely by intrinsic states), they may still possess different \"broad\" contents because broad content includes these extrinsic environmental factors [Source: computational-mind][Source: mental-representation].\n\nHowever, the slogan does **not** commit one to the view that thoughts themselves are physically located outside of the head. As noted in the sources, external individuation is not the same as external location. Just as a sunburn can be externally individuated (caused by the sun) but remains located on the skin, mental states with broad content remain situated within the individual's mind/brain even though their *content* is defined by relations to things outside it [Source: content-externalism].\n\n---\n*Verification note: these statements could not be confirmed against your sources — treat them as unverified:*\n- meanings just ain't in the head\n- It asserts that the individuation of mental content is not a function solely of an individual’s internal properties.",
