      "corpora_hit": [
        "sep"
      ],
      "vector_eligible": true,
      "synth": {
        "answer": "David Hume and Immanuel Kant offer fundamentally different accounts of causation, diverging on its origin, nature, and epistemic status. For Hume, causality is a psychological habit derived from experience; for Kant, it is an *a priori* category of the understanding required to structure experience itself.\n\n### Hume’s Account: Causation as Custom\nHume argues that all reasonings concerning matters of fact are founded on the relation of cause and effect (hume). He posits that we cannot discover any \"necessary connection\" or power in objects through observation alone. When we observe one event following another, we see only their constant conjunction—sequence and contiguity—not any glue or force binding them together [Source: hume]. \n\nConsequently, Hume reduces the idea of causality to a subjective feeling arising from custom or habit. After repeated observations of A followed by B, the mind forms an association such that the appearance of A leads us to expect B [Source: hegel-dialectics; Source: kant-hume-causality]. This is what Saul Kripke calls a \"skeptical solution\": Hume does not deny that causation exists as a pattern in our experience, but he denies it has any objective, necessary ontological status independent of human psychology (hume). For Hume, causal necessity is merely psychological regularity grounded in sensory items and association (kant-transcendental).\n\n### Kant’s Account: Causation as a Category\nKant rejects Hume’s reduction of causality to subjective custom. He argues that if causality were merely derived from experience, it could only yield contingent generalizations, yet we treat causal laws (like those of Newton) as having strict universality and necessity [Source: sep; Source: kant-hume-causality]. \n\nKant proposes that causality is not extracted *from* experience but is instead an *a priori* \"category of the understanding\" that makes experience possible [Source: induction-problem; Source: sep]. According to this view, the mind actively structures sensory data using concepts like cause and effect. Without these categories, our perceptions would remain a chaotic flux of subjective impressions rather than objective events in time [Source: sep]. Kant’s solution involves a \"Copernican revolution\": just as Copernicus reversed the relationship between the earth and sun, Kant argues we must assume that objects must conform to our cognitive faculties for us to have knowledge of them (hegel-dialectics). Thus, causality is a necessary condition for the possibility of experience itself.\n\n### Why Kant Saw Hume’s Problem as Demanding a Transcendental Solution\nKant famously reported that reading Hume woke him from his \"dogmatic slumbers\" (hume). He took Hume seriously because Hume correctly identified that causal necessity cannot be derived empirically [Source: sep]. However, Kant believed Hume stopped short by concluding that this meant causality was merely subjective habit. \n\nFor Kant, science and metaphysics require *synthetic a priori* judgments—propositions that are both informative about the world and necessarily true independent of specific experiences [Source: induction-problem; Source: kant-hume-causality]. If Hume were right that we can only know what experience provides, then scientific knowledge would collapse into skepticism. To save the universality and necessity required for science, Kant argued that reason must look to its own structures (transcendental conditions) rather than external objects [Source: hegel-dialectics].\n\nKant viewed Hume’s \"faculty of association\" as insufficient because mere sensory association cannot account for the strict universality found in causal propositions (kant-transcendental). Therefore, a transcendental solution was demanded: one had to demonstrate how concepts like causality are preconditions for any coherent experience. By grounding causality in the structure of human rationality rather than in the world-in-itself or in passive habit, Kant aimed to provide a complete solution to the doubt Hume raised about the foundations of scientific knowledge (kant-hume-causality; Source: sep).\n\nI don't have reliable information on whether contemporary philosophy considers Kant's specific resolution of the problem of induction superior to modern Bayesian approaches, but I can search for current debates on this if you would like.",
        "reasoning_chars": 0,
