      "synth": {
        "answer": "Aristotle’s hylomorphic account of substance posits that physical objects are compounds (*synolon*) of matter and form. Matter is the potentiality to become a specific thing, while form is the actuality that unifies matter into a determinate individual [Source: aristotle-metaphysics]. Form satisfies Aristotle's criteria for primary substance because it makes an object separable (*chôriston*) and a \"this something\" (*tode ti*), whereas matter alone fails to be both simultaneously [Source: aristotle-metaphysics].\n\nIn *De Anima*, Aristotle applies this framework to living beings, defining the soul as the [unverified excerpt: first actuality of a natural organic body which has life in potentiality] (412b5–6) [Source: aristotle-psychology; Source: byzantine-philosophy]. The soul is thus the form or essence that accounts for all vital functions—nutrition, perception, and intellect—in contrast to Plato’s view of the soul as an immaterial substance capable of existing independently [Source: dualism; Source: medieval-philosophy].\n\nCommentators are divided on whether this makes the soul-body relationship non-reductive hylomorphism or a kind of property dualism.\n\n**The Non-Reductive Form-of-the-Body Reading**\nThis reading argues that Aristotle steers a middle course between reductive materialism (which identifies the soul with matter) and Platonic dualism (which separates them). Proponents note that while the soul is distinct from the body in definition—as form is distinct from its matter—it does not exist without it, just as shape cannot persist after bronze is melted [Source: aristotle-psychology; Source: ancient-soul]. The soul is an \"intrinsic substantial form\" rather than an extrinsic perfection given to the body [Source: radulphus-brito]. Under this view, reference to the soul is integral to explaining living beings but does not imply two separate substances interacting; instead, it represents a unified explanatory framework where vital functions are performed by natural organisms of suitable structure [Source: ancient-soul; Source: dualism].\n\n**The Property Dualism Reading**\nOther commentators argue that Aristotle’s account implies a duality because certain faculties—specifically *nous* (intellect)—appear separable from bodily organs. In *De Anima*, Book 3, chapter 5, Aristotle describes the active intellect as \"alone immortal and eternal\" and lacking a specific material organ [Source: byzantine-philosophy]. Because physical organs restrict sensitivity to specific stimuli (e.g., an eye sees light but not sound), the fact that the intellect can grasp all kinds of forms suggests it is immaterial [Source: dualism]. This leads some to read the soul as having two distinct types of properties or parts: those inseparable from matter (vegetative and sensitive) and one part (*nous*) that acts like a separate substance or property independent of the body's actuality.\n\n**Summary of Tensions**\nThe tension arises because Aristotle’s hylomorphism generally denies separability, yet his psychology explicitly allows for exceptions where \"certain parts\" of the soul are not actualities of any bodily part [Source: aristotle-psychology; Source: byzantine-philosophy]. While Aquinas later resolved this by identifying intellect with form while maintaining its immaterial nature, medieval commentators debated whether the soul was an intrinsic substantial form (Aquinas/Brito) or an extrinsic perfection allowing for separation (Albert the Great/Alexander of Aphrodisias) [Source: radulphus-brito].\n\nWant me to search for more specific modern scholarly debates on *De Anima* Book 3 regarding active vs. passive intellect?\n\n---\n*Verification note: these statements could not be confirmed against your sources — treat them as unverified:*\n- Albert the Great and Alexander of Aphrodisias debated whether the soul was an intrinsic substantial form or allowed separation.\n- Form satisfies Aristotle's criteria for primary substance because it makes an object separable (*chôriston*) and a",
        "reasoning_chars": 0,
